<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa Igia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Santa Gilla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa Igia was the capital of the medieval Judicate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, on the edge of the lagoon west of present-day Cagliari. The judges ruled from there with their palace and cathedral from about the 11th century. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> conquered the judicate in 1258 it razed the town, and Pisan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-di-castro">Castel di Castro</a>, the core of modern Cagliari, replaced it.</p></div>
